.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_CREATE 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_create \- create a fixed-size memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_mempool *ccol_mempool_create(size_t elem_count,
                        size_t elem_size,
                        bool fallback_to_dynamic_memory,
                        bool single_threaded,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
                        char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a memory pool that manages a fixed number of fixed-size elements.
Every allocation returns an element of the same size. The pool gives O(1)
allocation and deallocation with a free list.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
elem_count                 Number of elements in the pool (must be > 0)
elem_size                  Size of each element in bytes. It must be > 0.
                          The minimum is sizeof(uintptr_t). The maximum
                          is SIZE_MAX less the stride rounding
fallback_to_dynamic_memory If true, allocate from the heap when the pool
                          is empty
single_threaded           If true, do not lock (faster but not
                          thread-safe)
mmgmt_procs               Custom memory management, or NULL for the
                          default
err                       Optional pointer that receives an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the new memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The stride between elements is elem_size rounded up to 16 bytes. The
library then rounds it on up to a power of two, unless it was built with
-DCCOL_MEMPOOL_COMPACT_LAYOUT=1. See doc/building.md in the source
tree. In both cases every element is aligned for any object type,
and not only the first one. This matches what malloc() guarantees. Any
object that you can store in heap memory can go into an entry
If elem_size < sizeof(uintptr_t), the library rounds it up
Returns NULL with an error in two cases. The first is an elem_size that
would overflow size_t after the stride rounding. The second is an
elem_count and a stride that together would overflow size_t. A pool
holds one stride for each element, then one status byte for each
element, all in one block
With the fallback on, an allocation from the pool never fails until the
system runs out of memory
The caller must destroy the pool with ccol_mempool_destroy()
The pool calls the functions of mmgmt_procs for its dynamic fallback
entries, and for its own bookkeeping of them, in the middle of an update
of its own state, with its lock held when it has one. Those functions
must therefore not call back into this same pool: with a lock the call
deadlocks, and without one it corrupts that state. An allocator built on
a pool must draw from a different pool

Sizing for performance:

Give a thread-safe pool at least 8 elements. Below that there is no room
for a cache for each thread. Every allocation and every free then takes
the lock of the pool, and concurrent callers serialise against one
another. 8 is a floor and not a target. The cache size grows with the
pool, so a pool sized for real use does much better than one sized at
exactly 8.

A pool gives out at most one cache for every 8 elements, and at most 16
in all. A thread that arrives when every cache is taken works through
the lock of the pool: one lock for each allocation and each free, which
is what a pool without a cache costs. It gets a cache as soon as a
thread that holds one exits. Size a pool that many threads share so
that each of them gets a cache.

A pool that receives a cache allocates a reserve above elem_count. The
reserve keeps elem_count available to any thread, no matter how many
entries other threads hold in their own caches. It costs memory. That
cost is at most half of elem_count. It falls to a quarter at 8192
elements, an eighth at 16384, and to a few percent or less from about
65536 upward. The reserve stops growing once a cache reaches its maximum
depth, so a large pool pays proportionally far less than a small one.

ccol_mempool_total_capacity() reports elem_count.
ccol_mempool_used_count() counts only the entries that the pool truly
handed out. It does not count an entry that the pool merely pulled into
a cache. What the reserve does change is how exact the upper end of that
count is under concurrent load. An entry leaves the cache of a thread
with no lock held, and so with no count to consult. Entries therefore go
into a cache before anybody knows whether they are needed. That decision
favours the guarantee above. In exchange, concurrent callers can hold a
few entries more than elem_count at one time, for a short time. They
never hold more than elem_count plus the reserve. The memory is
allocated when the pool is created in either case, so nothing grows at
run time. See ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3).

A thread gets a cache only once it holds a value on a thread-specific
key of the library, which is what lets the library free that cache when
the thread exits. A thread for which that fails works through the lock
of the pool, as above, and asks again at its next miss. A process that
has no thread-specific key left to give, and a library that is being
unloaded, give no pool a cache.

A pool created with single_threaded = true never uses the cache.
Neither does a pool created by
ccol_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(). The reserve and the
8-element floor do not apply to them.

The right elem_count, elem_size and single_threaded setting depend on
three things. They are the access pattern, the number of threads, and the
object lifetime of the program that uses the pool. No single choice is
best for all of them. Measure rather than assume. "make bench" in the source tree
runs the benchmarks of the pool beside the system allocator, on the
current machine.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when single_threaded is false. The pool then uses a mutex.

On the common path a thread-safe pool takes no lock at all. Each thread
keeps a small private cache of entries. It reaches the shared free list
only to refill or flush that cache. Threads that allocate and free at
the same time therefore do not contend with one another, and throughput
rises as you add threads.

An entry in the cache of one thread returns to the shared free list when
that thread exits. A destroy of the pool frees its whole block, those
entries included. The allocation of the cache is a separate matter.
ccol_mempool_destroy(3) gives the points at which that allocation goes
back to your allocator. Join a thread before you destroy a pool that it
used. That is enough, because the cleanup of a thread runs before
pthread_join() returns.

A single_threaded = true pool has no lock and no cache. Never use such a
pool from more than one thread.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
